  "question": {
    "text": "In a Young&#39;s double slit experiment, a laser light of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>560</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>nm</mi></math> produces an interference pattern with consecutive bright fringes&#39; separation of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>7</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>mm</mi></math>. Now another light is used to produce an interference pattern with consecutive bright fringes&#39; separation of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>8</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>mm</mi></math>. The wavelength of second light is _____&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>nm</mi><mo>.</mo></math>"
  },
  "options": [],
  "correct_answer": "630",
  "solution": {
    "text": "<blockquote><p>Given here:&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>560</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math>,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>&#946;</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math>&nbsp;and&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>&#946;</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math>.</p><p>Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>d</mi></math> be the width and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>D</mi></math> be the distance between slit and screen then fringe width,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#946;</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>&#955;</mi><mi>D</mi></mrow><mi>d</mi></mfrac></math>.</p><p>Thus, from above,&nbsp;&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#946;</mi><mo>&#8733;</mo><mi>&#955;</mi></math></p><p>So, the wavelength of second light is&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>&#946;</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>&#946;</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mfrac><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>9</mn><mn>8</mn></mfrac><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8756;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><msub><mi>&#955;</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>9</mn><mn>8</mn></mfrac><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>560</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>630</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>nm</mi><mo>.</mo></math></p></blockquote>"
  }
}